WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.850
As aggravating as it can be when a trailer
spoils a movie, it's worse when a poster does

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:05.850
it.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:10.000
Their designers only have one job: to create
a single compelling picture that gets audiences

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.360
excited to see a film.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.600
But it doesn't always work out.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.430
Here are some posters that somehow managed
to spoil the entire movie.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.430
Kiefer Alert

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:23.600
2003's Phone Booth is the story of a man played
by Colin Farrell who stands inside a phone

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.960
booth for the better part of a day, and for
almost the entire film.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.099
Surprising as it may sound, that premise leads
to a pretty interesting story, because he's

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:35.389
a hostage who's being told to stay inside
- or else - by a voice on the other end of

00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:36.449
the line.

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:39.350
That's the movie's central mystery: who's
the caller?

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.469
With all this hype surrounding the mystery
man, it'd be pretty silly if the promotional

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:48.680
material revealed his identity, and yet that's
exactly what happened: Kiefer Sutherland is

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.989
featured prominently in one version of Phone
Booth's poster, as well as the DVD case, looking

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:55.829
very suspicious.

00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:59.949
If you saw the poster before you saw the flick,
the experience of watching Phone Booth is

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:04.110
reduced to waiting around for Sutherland to
finally show up as the villain at the very

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.339
end, as promised.

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:07.710
"Isn't it funny?

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:12.740
You hear a phone ring, and it could be anybody."

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.289
Ender's ending

00:01:14.289 --> 00:01:18.679
This particular Ender's Game poster spoils
the finale of the film by showing the climax's

00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:20.460
most important shot.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.820
Coupled with even a basic synopsis of the
film, this gives it all away.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:29.369
2013's Ender's Game is the story of a kid
named Ender who must train to prevent an alien

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:30.640
invasion.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.390
Couple that knowledge with the poster's image
of Ender blowing up a planet with a giant

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.039
pair of mega-lasers, and there really aren't
a lot of questions left on the table.

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:41.219
"If we destroy the planet, we destroy the
queens."

00:01:41.219 --> 00:01:45.399
And even if one doesn't immediately connect
the dots before starting the film, the poster's

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:49.729
true nature will eventually reveal itself
and still guarantee that the ending is spoiled

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:51.810
way ahead of time.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.229
Fun, terminated

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:58.979
While the second trailer for 2015's Terminator
Genisys gave away this big spoiler as well,

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:03.090
trailers are far easier to avoid than a poster,
which reveals its contents the second you

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.090
see it.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.690
The designers of this Terminator Genisys poster
chose to reveal that its "hero," John Connor,

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.650
is actually a Terminator and, by extension,
the villain - a twist that doesn't creep up

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.360
until halfway through the movie.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.090
Worse yet, this is the movie's only real twist.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.450
It's not like the marketing team had anything
else up their sleeves when they revealed this

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:22.450
bombshell.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.500
This is the movie's sole hook - leading some
to speculate that the poster was just a desperate

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.349
attempt to garner excitement for the film
even at the risk of cannibalizing its only

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:32.260
thread of intrigue.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.260
Throwing the fight

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:38.870
1985's Rocky IV allegorically speaks to Cold
War tensions between the United States and

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.069
the Soviet Union.

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:45.110
World champion Rocky Balboa represents the
U.S., while the unfeeling Ivan Drago represents

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.220
the Russians.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.040
It's about as clear-cut as a movie can get,
in terms of setting up an obvious good guy

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.069
and bad guy.

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:54.739
Given that dynamic, no one should have any
questions about who is going to win the big

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:55.890
fight at the end.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:03:00.290
However, in case there was any lingering doubt
in a prospective viewer's mind - Rocky did

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.620
lose at the end of the first film, after all
- this poster cleared that confusion right

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:05.620
up.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.250
Featuring the title character cheering, bruised,
and draped in an American flag, the poster

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:12.590
puts the film's ending front and center for
all to see.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.340
No twist in Thailand

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.500
For the most part, the marketing for 2016's
10 Cloverfield Lane was very carefully crafted

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.019
to maintain the movie's sense of mystery.

00:03:22.019 --> 00:03:26.129
Audiences were left to wonder why John Goodman
was so intent on keeping Mary Elizabeth Winstead

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:27.580
trapped in his bunker.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:31.260
Did it have something to do with the Cloverfield
monster from the last movie with "Cloverfield"

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.260
in the title?

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.659
No one knew - at least until an official poster
came out in Thailand and blatantly spoiled

00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:37.829
the big finale.

00:03:37.829 --> 00:03:41.810
It depicts an alien spacecraft, with Winstead
booking it away from the ship in a run for

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:42.920
her life.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.420
This poster spoiled the massive twist at the
end of the movie - as well as the rest of

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.040
the story leading up to that point - all with
a single image.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.640
"Oh boy.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.250
That's bad."

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.250
Thanks for watching!

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:56.569
